<p>Hello again! how are you doing?</p>
<p>Thanks for the response, especially the bit about the ending.</p>
<p>The ending definitely does need something more than just lost hope. I like bleak endings, but they only really work when there's a lesson learned, or if it affects more than just the main character. Right now, it seems like the ending doesn't have a payoff and only affects the main character, which thankfully you helped bring to my attention. I think as you recommended, the ending should be bittersweet instead of bleak.<br />
I started to brainstorm a different idea for the ending:<br />
Instead of just all hope being lost and he transforms, The Oneiroi Collective gives him an offer: he can be placed in an endless dream, which would replicate reality completely, with one catch: it takes place only in the summer of 1965, and only in his home town. With no other option, Ty takes the offer, leaving his physical body (which now completely transforms into an instance of the SCP) behind. He falls asleep for the last time.</p>
<p>Of course, I'll probably brainstorm more ending ideas that may work better.</p>
<p>Thanks again for the critique, and I hope you have had a merry Christmas and a great day.</p>
<p>Sincerely, Gravy.</p>

